{"candidates":[{"file":"src/users/users.service.ts","symbol":"UsersService.create / UsersService.update","start_line":1,"end_line":200,"explanation":"I could not open the repository, so this is an inference and the line range is approximate. Registration and profile update both write the user through UsersService. Both symptoms (lastName becoming firstName on create and on update) point to code they share. The most likely defect is a persistence or mapping step that assigns `lastName: clonedPayload.firstName` or `lastName = firstName`. It would be a copy-paste slip when building the user object."},{"file":"src/auth/auth.service.ts","symbol":"AuthService.register","start_line":1,"end_line":300,"explanation":"Unverified. register() builds the create payload from AuthRegisterLoginDto and passes it to usersService.create. If it sets `lastName: dto.firstName`, new users get a duplicated first name. It would not explain the profile-update symptom, so it is less likely than the shared user service."},{"file":"src/auth/dto/auth-register-login.dto.ts","symbol":"AuthRegisterLoginDto.lastName","start_line":6,"end_line":50,"explanation":"Unverified. A faulty `@Transform` or the wrong property on the `lastName` field could copy firstName into lastName during validation. The same pattern may be repeated in the profile update DTO."}],"fix":{"file":"src/users/users.service.ts","description":"Find where lastName is assigned from firstName, for example `lastName: dto.firstName`. Change it to `lastName: dto.lastName` in both the create and update paths. Do the same in AuthService.register and in any DTO transform. Then add a test that registers 'Jane Smith' and checks that lastName stays 'Smith' after registration and after a profile update."}}
